            "lens": "review-resilience",
            "findings": [
              {
                "id": "R4-001",
                "lens": "resilience",
                "location": "engine/review/fsstore/bypath.go:46-49",
                "severity": "WARNING",
                "claim": "On non-linux platforms a directory flush failure after the link is reported as a plain failure, not ErrNotFlushed. A caller retrying after that error would create a second copy of an already-published result (next number). The candidate documents this limit in the comment, but the retry-duplication failure mode remains on those platforms.",
                "proof_refs": [
                  "engine/review/fsstore/bypath.go:46-49",
                  "engine/review/app/service.go:167-173"
                ],
                "evidence_class": "inferential",
                "causal_disposition": "pre-existing"
              }
            ],
            "evidence": [
              "Reviewed all 16 manifest paths. The core change maps a post-link directory flush failure to app.ErrNotFlushed (held_linux.go) and Service.Record/store treat it as stored with exit 0 and a stderr warning, which avoids duplicate results on retry and is covered by tests at the app, cmd and fsstore levels.",
              "cmd/review.go handoff write failure now prints an error and exits 1 while the result is already stored; the message names the stored path, so the operator is told not to redo it.",
              "store() returns on ErrNotFlushed without retrying, so the number is not consumed twice; the error wrapping uses multiple %w which requires Go 1.20+, within the module's go 1.21.",
              "The non-linux limitation is the only residual resilience gap; it is documented in code and CHANGELOG and was the baseline behavior."
            ]
          }
        }
