              {
                "id": "R2-001",
                "lens": "readability",
                "location": "engine/cmd/review.go:258",
                "severity": "WARNING",
                "claim": "The handoff-write failure path in reportRecorded builds its message by wrapping an fmt.Errorf inside a Fprintf and calling p.exit(1) directly, duplicating what fail() does for the same message shape. If fail() changes its prefix or format, this copy will silently drift. The reason fail() was bypassed (probably to avoid a second wrapper or a different exit path) is not documented.",
                "proof_refs": [
                  "engine/cmd/review.go:258-259"
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "R2-002",
                "lens": "readability",
                "location": "engine/review/fsstore/held_linux.go:142-150",
                "severity": "WARNING",
                "claim": "openBelow now wraps errors in hand-built fs.PathError values to mimic the wording os.ReadDir and os.Mkdir used before, with a conditional that excludes ENOTDIR/ELOOP for the non-create case. This error-text compatibility logic is subtle and implicit: the Op names ('mkdir', 'open') are synthesized rather than coming from the failing call, and the double wrapping ('make path: mkdir path: ...') repeats the path. A short comment explains only the read case.",
                "proof_refs": [
                  "engine/review/fsstore/held_linux.go:142-150"
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "R2-003",
                "lens": "readability",
                "location": "engine/review/fsstore/bypath.go:46-49",
                "severity": "SUGGESTION",
                "claim": "The portable store now documents that a flush failure after the link is reported as a plain failure rather than ErrNotFlushed, so the same condition yields different Record outcomes (exit 0 with a warning on linux, a failure on other platforms, where a retry would store a second copy). The divergence is only recorded in a comment; callers and the CHANGELOG describe the linux behavior only partially.",
                "proof_refs": [
                  "engine/review/fsstore/bypath.go:46-49",
                  "CHANGELOG.md:9"
                ]
              }
            ],
            "evidence": [
