            "evidence": [
              "Reviewed all 16 changed paths. The flush-failure path is consistent end to end: held_linux.go wraps app.ErrNotFlushed after the link, store() in service.go stops retrying and returns that error (so a published result is not stored a second time), Record returns Recorded.NotFlushed with a nil error, and reportRecorded prints the warning and exits 0 with the skeleton.",
              "Tests cover each layer: record_test.go checks Record with an unflushed store (nil error, Path and Number set, NotFlushed wraps ErrNotFlushed, Create called once); held_linux_test.go checks the held create against an injected flush failure and that the file is stored with its content; review_test.go checks exit 0, non-empty stdout and the stderr wording.",
              "The write-failure branch of reportRecorded still exits 1 and names the stored path, matching the earlier behavior. The inline replacement of fail() keeps the same message shape.",
              "The Names and Create error-wording changes are pinned by exact-string tests, and the temporaryName helper is checked against isTemporary.",
              "Observation only: on non-linux platforms a directory flush failure is still reported as a plain failure and not as ErrNotFlushed, so a retry could store a second copy. bypath.go and the CHANGELOG state this limit openly, and it is the existing behavior, so it is not a regression."
